{"text": " What will I do for the code? First, I will make a function. I will give the name of the function Public static. It has a return type int. Why int? Because I am going to return the total number of ways. I will give the name of this function. Tiling problem. There will be one parameter in it, my integer n. Why is this integer n? Because my size of the floor is equal to 2xn. 2xn is my floor size. So, n is the sign that the length of my floor is going to be equal to the length of my floor. Now, what I have to do first? First of all, I will do my work. What is the work in my recursive function? The work is to take a choice. Let us assume that first of all, I have taken a vertical choice. As soon as I have taken a vertical choice, what do I have to calculate? I have taken a vertical choice and sold one tile. Now, I just have to calculate this. How many tiles will be in n minus 1? Int vertical tiles is equal to... I will call this same function to the tiling problem for n minus 1. So, I have taken a choice here. After placing the vertical, how many tiles will be in n minus 1? I have stored this in vertical tiles. In fact, instead of vertical tiles, I will write fn minus 1. That means, I will get so many choices. Now, what will I do? I will take a horizontal choice. Horizontal choice. As soon as I have taken a horizontal choice, I will have to calculate fn minus 2. What will happen? This will be the tiling problem for n minus 2. So, the horizontal choice will be left here and the vertical choice will be left here. What will be my total ways? Int total ways is equal to... My fn minus 1 will return and fn minus 2 will return. And finally, with the same function, I will return my total ways. What can I do on top? I can write my base case on top. My base case will be that if my value of n is 0 or my value of n is 1, then I have only one way left. So, I will return 1. I hope this thing will be clear. We discussed this in the base case first. The work is to make a choice. As soon as we make a choice, then the tiling problem for n minus 1 will be solved. The tiling problem for n minus 2 will be solved. Here, I am faith that n minus 1 and n minus 2 will be the right answer. I will calculate the total ways and then I will return the total ways. Let's do this safely. We will put a call from the main function. I will pass the tiling problem and here I will pass my n as 3. I know that the answer I should get for 3 should be equal to 3. Now, if I put a call for n is equal to 2, then the answer should be equal to 2. One is that we will place both horizontally and one is that we will place both vertically. If I put a call for n is equal to 4, then what should happen? I will get the answer 5. This is a homework problem for you to figure out. You have to solve it on a paper pen. If the size of the floor is equal to 2 cross 4, then how do I get 5 ways? What are the 5 ways in which I am placing my tiles? We have to solve this right now. The most important thing about this question is that it will not come only for 2 cross 1. What will happen in the question? This question can also come for 4 cross 1. How will I get the question? I will get the question that the size of the floor is equal to 4 cross n. So, my floor will look something like this. This side will always be 4 and this side will always be n. After that, what will be the size of the tile? We will get the size of the tile equal to 4 cross 1. So, there is no need to apply the same formula. The formula will look exactly the same. If you have this question for 4 cross 1, then it will also come. Along with this, there will be a lot of questions in the recursion, in which one thing is used called DP. DP means Dynamic Programming. Dynamic Programming means that we are solving the same question again and again. Instead of calculating the same thing again and again, what do we do? We store the answer somewhere. So, here, because we have only learned basic recursion, then there will be some questions that we will solve from the recursion app, but it will not have the most optimized solution. Its time complexity will be quite large. So, when we go to the end and learn Dynamic Programming, after that we can solve all these questions in a maximum optimized way. Till then, we have to wait a little because we are only working on the logic building, we are building the logic of the recursion. So, we are not doing the most optimized solution. We are learning the recursion now that how is the recursion working? We have understood the logic of the recursion. So, the questions of the graph will be very easy. The questions of the linked list will be very easy. There will be a lot more things that will be easy. In fact, the concept of Dynamic Programming, we will be understanding that very well in the end. So, right now, the most optimized solution may not be available to you. There are a lot of problems. But yes, you will get the most logical solution. How to understand them, how to visualize them.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.0, "text": " What will I do for the code? First, I will make a function. I will give the name of the function", "tokens": [50364, 708, 486, 286, 360, 337, 264, 3089, 30, 2386, 11, 286, 486, 652, 257, 2445, 13, 286, 486, 976, 264, 1315, 295, 264, 2445, 50564], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.34207332026851067, "compression_ratio": 1.7444933920704846, "no_speech_prob": 0.09797381609678268}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 4.0, "end": 10.0, "text": " Public static. It has a return type int. Why int? Because I am going to return the total number of ways.", "tokens": [50564, 9489, 13437, 13, 467, 575, 257, 2736, 2010, 560, 13, 1545, 560, 30, 1436, 286, 669, 516, 281, 2736, 264, 3217, 1230, 295, 2098, 13, 50864], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.34207332026851067, "compression_ratio": 1.7444933920704846, "no_speech_prob": 0.09797381609678268}, {"id": 2, "seek": 0, "start": 10.0, "end": 17.0, "text": " I will 